issue_context.body:
## Issue Information

In OpenSSL 3.0 **Engine** framework, the reference number of _ECX_KEY_ will be decreased twice when calling the _EVP_PKEY_free()_ API, which will cause a core dump as shown below.
``` bash
> /usr/local/ssl3/bin/openssl speed -engine qatengine -elapsed -async_jobs 128  ecdhx25519
Engine "qatengine" set.
You have chosen to measure elapsed time instead of user CPU time.
Doing 253 bits  ecdh's for 10s: 1263043 253-bits ECDH ops in 10.00s
version: 3.0.8
built on: Tue Mar 14 04:56:11 2023 UTC
options: bn(64,64)
compiler: gcc -fPIC -pthread -m64 -Wa,--noexecstack -Wall -O0 -g -DOPENSSL_USE_NODELETE -DL_ENDIAN -DOPENSSL_PIC -DOPENSSL_BUILDING_OPENSSL
CPUINFO: OPENSSL_ia32cap=0x7ffef3f7ffebffff:0x40417f5ef3bfb7ef
                              op      op/s
 253 bits ecdh (X25519)   0.0000s 126304.3
crypto/ec/ecx_key.c:68: OpenSSL internal error: refcount error
[1]    1095734 abort (core dumped)  /usr/local/ssl3/bin/openssl speed -engine qatengine -elapsed -async_jobs 128
```
## Root cause

To explain briefly, this is caused by the fact that the number of references to _ECX_KEY_ is reduced twice during the call of the _EVP_PKEY_free()_ function.

Below I will explain by comparing the 3.0 version of OpenSSL and the 1.1.1 version of OpenSSL.

### OpenSSL 1.1.1
In OpenSSL 1.1.1 the references number will be decreased and only be decreased in _EVP_PKEY_free()_. After that, It will call _EVP_PKEY_free_it()_ to free the pkey. In the _EVP_PKEY_free_it()_ function, it will call the _ecx_free()_ method via a function pointer _x->ameth->pkey_free()_, which is actually points to _ecx_free()_. 

In OpenSSL 1.1.1, _ecx_free()_ will just clean and free the memory of pkey, it will not decrease the reference number of pkey.

For your convenience, I have pasted all relevant functions below for reference.
``` C
void EVP_PKEY_free(EVP_PKEY *x)
{
    int i;

    if (x == NULL)
        return;
    // The references will be decreased here
    CRYPTO_DOWN_REF(&x->references, &i, x->lock);
    REF_PRINT_COUNT("EVP_PKEY", x);
    if (i > 0)
        return;
    REF_ASSERT_ISNT(i < 0);
    EVP_PKEY_free_it(x);
    CRYPTO_THREAD_lock_free(x->lock);
    sk_X509_ATTRIBUTE_pop_free(x->attributes, X509_ATTRIBUTE_free);
    OPENSSL_free(x);
}
```
``` C
static void EVP_PKEY_free_it(EVP_PKEY *x)
{
    /* internal function; x is never NULL */
    if (x->ameth && x->ameth->pkey_free) {
        x->ameth->pkey_free(x); // will call ecx_free()
        x->pkey.ptr = NULL;
    }
#ifndef OPENSSL_NO_ENGINE
    ENGINE_finish(x->engine);
    x->engine = NULL;
    ENGINE_finish(x->pmeth_engine);
    x->pmeth_engine = NULL;
#endif
}
```

```C
static void ecx_free(EVP_PKEY *pkey)
{
    if (pkey->pkey.ecx != NULL)
        OPENSSL_secure_clear_free(pkey->pkey.ecx->privkey, KEYLEN(pkey));
    OPENSSL_free(pkey->pkey.ecx);
}
```

``` C
void CRYPTO_secure_clear_free(void *ptr, size_t num,
                              const char *file, int line)
{
#ifdef OPENSSL_SECURE_MEMORY
    size_t actual_size;

    if (ptr == NULL)
        return;
    if (!CRYPTO_secure_allocated(ptr)) {
        OPENSSL_cleanse(ptr, num);
        CRYPTO_free(ptr, file, line);
        return;
    }
    CRYPTO_THREAD_write_lock(sec_malloc_lock);
    actual_size = sh_actual_size(ptr);
    CLEAR(ptr, actual_size);
    secure_mem_used -= actual_size;
    sh_free(ptr);
    CRYPTO_THREAD_unlock(sec_malloc_lock);
#else
    if (ptr == NULL)
        return;
    OPENSSL_cleanse(ptr, num);
    CRYPTO_free(ptr, file, line);
#endif /* OPENSSL_SECURE_MEMORY */
}
```

### OpenSSL 3.0

The call stack of _EVP_PKEY_free()_ is similar to that of OpenSSL 1.1.1 (_EVP_PKEY_free()_ -> _evp_pkey_free_it()_ -> _evp_pkey_free_legacy()_ ->  _ecx_free()_ -> _ossl_ecx_key_free()_ ), but the content encapsulated in the _ecx_free()_ function called by the function pointer _ameth->pkey_free()_ has changed a lot.

The _ecx_free()_ will call _ossl_ecx_key_free()_, which will decrease the reference number of _ECX_KEY_ **again**!

``` C
static void ecx_free(EVP_PKEY *pkey)
{
    ossl_ecx_key_free(pkey->pkey.ecx);
}

void ossl_ecx_key_free(ECX_KEY *key)
{
    int i;

    if (key == NULL)
        return;
    // The references number of key is decreased again!
    CRYPTO_DOWN_REF(&key->references, &i, key->lock);
    REF_PRINT_COUNT("ECX_KEY", key);
    if (i > 0)
        return;
    REF_ASSERT_ISNT(i < 0);

    OPENSSL_free(key->propq);
    OPENSSL_secure_clear_free(key->privkey, key->keylen);
    CRYPTO_THREAD_lock_free(key->lock);
    OPENSSL_free(key);
}
```

``` C
static void evp_pkey_free_it(EVP_PKEY *x)
{
    /* internal function; x is never NULL */
    evp_keymgmt_util_clear_operation_cache(x, 1);
#ifndef FIPS_MODULE
    evp_pkey_free_legacy(x);
#endif

    if (x->keymgmt != NULL) {
        evp_keymgmt_freedata(x->keymgmt, x->keydata);
        EVP_KEYMGMT_free(x->keymgmt);
        x->keymgmt = NULL;
        x->keydata = NULL;
    }
    x->type = EVP_PKEY_NONE;
}
```

``` C
void EVP_PKEY_free(EVP_PKEY *x)
{
    int i;

    if (x == NULL)
        return;

    CRYPTO_DOWN_REF(&x->references, &i, x->lock);
    REF_PRINT_COUNT("EVP_PKEY", x);
    if (i > 0)
        return;
    REF_ASSERT_ISNT(i < 0);
    evp_pkey_free_it(x);
#ifndef FIPS_MODULE
    CRYPTO_free_ex_data(CRYPTO_EX_INDEX_EVP_PKEY, x, &x->ex_data);
#endif
    CRYPTO_THREAD_lock_free(x->lock);
#ifndef FIPS_MODULE
    sk_X509_ATTRIBUTE_pop_free(x->attributes, X509_ATTRIBUTE_free);
#endif
    OPENSSL_free(x);
}
```

``` C
void evp_pkey_free_legacy(EVP_PKEY *x)
{
    const EVP_PKEY_ASN1_METHOD *ameth = x->ameth;
    ENGINE *tmpe = NULL;

    if (ameth == NULL && x->legacy_cache_pkey.ptr != NULL)
        ameth = EVP_PKEY_asn1_find(&tmpe, x->type);

    if (ameth != NULL) {
        if (x->legacy_cache_pkey.ptr != NULL) {
            /*
             * We should never have both a legacy origin key, and a key in the
             * legacy cache.
             */
            assert(x->pkey.ptr == NULL);
            /*
             * For the purposes of freeing we make the legacy cache look like
             * a legacy origin key.
             */
            x->pkey = x->legacy_cache_pkey;
            x->legacy_cache_pkey.ptr = NULL;
        }
        if (ameth->pkey_free != NULL)
            ameth->pkey_free(x); // the EVP_KEY is double freed here when EVP_PKEY_free is called.
        x->pkey.ptr = NULL;
    }
# ifndef OPENSSL_NO_ENGINE
    ENGINE_finish(tmpe);
    ENGINE_finish(x->engine);
    x->engine = NULL;
    ENGINE_finish(x->pmeth_engine);
    x->pmeth_engine = NULL;
# endif
}
```

## Solution
To get around this, I think the easiest way is to remove the _CRYPTO_DOWN_REF()_ operation in the _ossl_ecx_key_free(ECX_KEY *key)_ function, so that the reference of _ECX_KEY_ will be decreased only one time in _ECX_PKEY_free()_ function.

Since I don't know the scope of the modified function, I'm not sure if such a modification makes sense.
Please give me some advice, thanks!

``` bash
diff --git a/crypto/ec/ecx_key.c b/crypto/ec/ecx_key.c
index dcec26c2e9..cad3e27b29 100644
--- a/crypto/ec/ecx_key.c
+++ b/crypto/ec/ecx_key.c
@@ -61,11 +61,13 @@ void ossl_ecx_key_free(ECX_KEY *key)
     if (key == NULL)
         return;

-    CRYPTO_DOWN_REF(&key->references, &i, key->lock);
-    REF_PRINT_COUNT("ECX_KEY", key);
-    if (i > 0)
-        return;
-    REF_ASSERT_ISNT(i < 0);
+    if (key->references > 0) {
+        CRYPTO_DOWN_REF(&key->references, &i, key->lock);
+        REF_PRINT_COUNT("ECX_KEY", key);
+        if (i > 0)
+            return;
+        REF_ASSERT_ISNT(i < 0);
+    }

     OPENSSL_free(key->propq);
     OPENSSL_secure_clear_free(key->privkey, key->keylen);
(END)
```

comments:
I'm failing to see the double free from the above description.

There are two different structures involved here. The ECX_KEY structure and the EVP_PKEY structure. They *both* have a references field:

```` C
struct ecx_key_st {
   ...
    CRYPTO_REF_COUNT references;
   ...
};
````
and 

```` C
struct evp_pkey_st {
    ...
    CRYPTO_REF_COUNT references;
    ...
} /* EVP_PKEY */ ;
````

So, an EVP_PKEY is a referenced counted object. It contains within it an ECX_KEY which is *also* reference counted.

The function `EVP_PKEY_free` decrements the references field of the EVP_PKEY structure and `ossl_ecx_key_free` decrements the references field of the ECX_KEY structure. That isn't a double free.

There must be some other reason for the refcount error being seen from the speed app in conjunction with the QAT engine.

Had the same problem a while ago with our engine.  The root cause was that the `ECX_KEY` structure has changed between 1.1.1 and 3.x, but the code was not properly changed:
OpenSSL 3.x:
```
typedef struct ecx_key_st {
    OSSL_LIB_CTX *libctx;
    char *propq;
    unsigned int haspubkey:1;
    unsigned char pubkey[MAX_KEYLEN];
    unsigned char *privkey;
    size_t keylen;
    ECX_KEY_TYPE type;
    CRYPTO_REF_COUNT references;
    CRYPTO_RWLOCK *lock;
} ECX_KEY;
```
versus OpenSSL 1.1.1:
```
typedef struct {
    unsigned char pub[57];
    unsigned char *priv;
} ECX_KEY;
```
So you might be able to check in the core dump if the engine uses the correct ECX_KEY definition.

After fixing this, our engine works fine with OpenSSL 3.0.  Of course, you cannot use the same engine you used for 1.1.1 in 3.0 and vice versa.
